# First-party image pins — the version of every first-party service prod runs.
#
# TRACKED IN GIT, deliberately (SERV-96). These values used to live inside the
# PROD_ENV_FILE environment secret alongside real credentials, which made a version
# change an unlogged secret edit and left promote/rollback (SERV-78, SERV-79) with no
# writable home for them: GITHUB_TOKEN has no `secrets` permission at all, so a
# workflow that repoints a tag by editing the secret needs a PAT with
# environment-secret write — the grant that 403'd in SGNT-29. Editing a tracked file
# needs `contents: write` and nothing else.
#
# The payoff beyond that: a version change is a reviewable diff, and rollback gets its
# index for free — `git log -p versions.env` is the record of what was pinned when.
#
# An image tag is not a credential, and nothing secret belongs in this file. Real
# secrets stay in PROD_ENV_FILE; see .env.example.
#
# ── Convention ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One variable per SOURCE REPO, not per image: a repo's backend and frontend ship from
# the same release and pin together, so these 12 variables cover 16 images. That count
# has gone stale twice — the mapping deploy-scope.sh uses is derived from the compose
# file and not from this sentence, so re-derive it rather than trusting the number.
#
#   major.minor  services with release-please versions       LYCEUM_TAG=1.10
#                Patch releases still flow in on a `docker compose pull` and nothing
#                else does — the one float kept deliberately, so a security fix lands
#                without an edit here.
#   a commit sha HISTORY. No pin below uses this form, and a new one should not.
#                argosy and drydock were pinned this way until SERV-125, and not
#                because they had no releases — they cut them normally the whole
#                time. Their publish workflows asked for the semver tags on
#                `on: push: tags`, and that trigger had never fired once, because
#                release-please cuts tags under GITHUB_TOKEN and GitHub creates
#                no workflow runs from events that token authored. So no
#                versioned image was ever built to pin to. Fixed upstream by
#                publishing from the release-please run instead.
#
# Both sha forms still need to be readable, because `git log -p versions.env` is the
# rollback index and it carries them: argosy's publish workflow tagged `sha-<short>`,
# drydock's the full 40-char commit sha with no prefix. Copy the form the registry
# actually carries rather than normalising them — a "tidied" tag that does not exist
# resolves to nothing and fails the deploy. verify-tag.sh checks whatever string it is
# given against the registry and does not care which form it is, so a promote across
# the two styles needs no special handling.
#
# Careful when auditing any of these: a `sha-<short>` tag and a release tag can be
# DIFFERENT images built from the SAME commit, because a release builds the same source
# twice seconds apart — once for the push to main, once for the release. Compare the
# org.opencontainers.image.revision label, not the digest, or you will invent version
# drift that is not there (SERV-88).
#
# ── How this reaches the stack ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# deploy.yml renders this file and PROD_ENV_FILE into a single $DEPLOY_ROOT/.env via
# scripts/render-env.sh, so the deployed .env stays the complete environment compose
# reads. It then asserts on `docker compose config --images` and fails the deploy if
# any first-party image still resolves to `:latest` or a bare `:`.

APERTURE_TAG=1.14
AMBER_TAG=0.8
ARGOSY_TAG=0.27
CENTRIFUGE_TAG=1.6
CHRONICLE_TAG=1.1
COOK_BOOK_TAG=1.1
DRYDOCK_TAG=1.7
INTERLOCK_TAG=1.4
LYCEUM_TAG=1.12
PLACARD_TAG=0.3
PURSER_TAG=0.16
SWITCHYARD_TAG=4.20
